---
import Crystal from '../components/Crystal.astro';
import '../styles/site.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const gh = 'https://github.com/CrystalBackup/CrystalBackup';
const links = {
  gh,
  home: `${base}/`,
  why: `${base}/#why`,
  how: `${base}/#how`,
  compare: `${base}/#compare`,
  roadmap: `${base}/#roadmap`,
  docs: `${base}/docs/`,
  testing: `${gh}/blob/main/spec/08-testing-and-dod.md`,
  security: `${gh}/blob/main/spec/03-security-and-tenancy.md`,
  adr: `${gh}/tree/main/spec/adr`,
  crucible: `${gh}/tree/main/test/crucible`,
  license: `${gh}/blob/main/LICENSE`,
};

const layers = [
  { layer: 'Unit', harness: 'go&nbsp;test&nbsp;-race — pure Go, no cluster', runs: 'every push', gate: 'merge' },
  { layer: 'Integration', harness: 'envtest — real API server + etcd, no kubelet', runs: 'every PR', gate: 'merge' },
  { layer: 'e2e core', harness: 'kind + csi-driver-hostpath + SeaweedFS', runs: 'every PR', gate: 'merge' },
  { layer: 'e2e full + fidelity', harness: 'same, extended matrix + metadata corpus', runs: 'nightly + release tags', gate: 'release' },
  { layer: 'Crucible', harness: 'disposable real platform — Ceph, Longhorn, Object Storage', runs: 'on demand', gate: 'milestone acceptance' },
];

// Every crucible acceptance run is published in full — passes, skips and the defects each
// round found. Files live in website/public/reports/, so they are served from `${base}/reports/`.
const reports = [
  {
    milestone: 'M1',
    covered: 'Core engine and cluster DR — the schedule → backup → per-namespace fan-out cascade, CSI snapshot exposers, discovery from the repository, retention',
    links: [['Crucible M1', 'crucible-m1.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: 'M2',
    covered: 'Restore — self-service in a namespace, operator-mediated cluster-DR restore, <code>ClusterRestore</code>, admission policies. Every restored volume byte-compared against the seed manifest',
    links: [
      ['Crucible M2', 'crucible-m2.html'],
      ['0.2.1 re-run', 'crucible-m2.1.html'],
      ['Audit 2.1', 'audit-m2.1.html'],
    ],
  },
  {
    milestone: 'M3',
    covered: 'Manifests and cluster-scoped DR — the sanitization engine, mode-aware apply, cluster-resource capture with opt-in and selective restore',
    links: [
      ['Crucible M3', 'crucible-m3.html'],
      ['Audit 3.1', 'audit-m3.1.html'],
      ['Audit 3.2', 'audit-m3.2.html'],
    ],
  },
  {
    milestone: 'M4',
    covered: 'Consistency hooks, repository verification (<code>restic&nbsp;check</code>) and scheduled maintenance. Run <strong>seven independent times</strong> — the milestone’s hardest bug was a snapshot leak reproducing about one run in three',
    links: [['Crucible M4', 'crucible-m4.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: 'M5',
    covered: 'Namespace plane, external sync, right to erasure — plus a full-suite non-regression pass on the build that shipped',
    links: [['Crucible M5', 'crucible-m5.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: 'M6',
    covered: 'Observability hardening — the metrics catalogue, eleven alert rules watched <em>firing</em> against a real Prometheus, traces, the self-check, and a restore-fidelity gate that compares a restore to its source file by file. The first report here that is <strong>not label-filtered</strong>: the whole suite, <strong>82 of 82 checks, nothing failed and nothing skipped</strong>',
    links: [['Crucible M6', 'crucible-m6.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: '0.6.1',
    covered: 'The first mover sizing this project ever shipped, put in front of real Ceph. Every mover Job had run BestEffort since M1 — no requests, no limits, an unbounded restic cache, no heap cap. The same full suite again on a fresh cluster: <strong>82 of 82, nothing failed and nothing skipped</strong>, on the built-in defaults, with zero evicted pods and zero OOM kills',
    links: [['Crucible 0.6.1', 'crucible-m6-1.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: '0.6.2',
    covered: 'The soak kit — the instrument for M6’s two-week soak, and mostly a release about the instrument being broken: the collector reported NOT_MEASURED for two of the four mover sizing classes beside a campaign that had run dozens of backups. A third full-suite pass, <strong>82 of 82, nothing failed and nothing skipped</strong>',
    links: [['Crucible 0.6.2', 'crucible-m6-2.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: '0.6.3',
    covered: 'The first campaign installed the way the documentation tells a reader to install. Four defects had blocked a real user’s first hour on their own cluster and none was reachable by any test here — two of them because the crucible’s own deploy script had configured around them before the first campaign ever ran. The suite also grew a lane that had never verified anything: <code>m6/stall</code> shipped red and took three runs to become evidence, and the operator was correct all three times. <strong>90 of 90, nothing failed and nothing skipped</strong>, in 2h43m',
    links: [['Crucible 0.6.3', 'crucible-m6-3.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: '0.6.4',
    covered: 'A campaign about a fix that was wrong twice before it was right. An Argo CD prune deleted the operator namespace on a real cluster, the credentials came back two minutes after the key, and the operator minted a fresh DEK over the only one that could open a repository holding 38 snapshots. It took three campaigns: one killed mid-run with no verdict, one that went <strong>87 passed / 1 failed / 2 skipped</strong> because the first cut of the guard blocked provisioning even where an in-cluster key already existed — <code>m6/alerts</code> caught it, by making a backup fail so early that the alert about failing backups could no longer see one. The suite itself did not change a line. <strong>90 of 90, nothing failed and nothing skipped</strong>, in 2h44m',
    links: [['Crucible 0.6.4', 'crucible-m6-4.html']],
  },
  {
    milestone: '0.6.5',
    covered: 'A campaign that failed first, and the failure is where the value was. One PVC on a real cluster named a StorageClass that did not exist — legal, for a statically provisioned volume — and it held the head of its namespace’s queue for <strong>thirty-one hours</strong>, during which a <code>Forbid</code> schedule skipped every night and the dashboard read 0% success while every counter around it was either silent or wrong. Twelve lots. Run 1 of the campaign then went <strong>62 passed / 7 failed / 21 never reached</strong>: one leaked <code>VolumeSnapshotContent</code> failed five specs in four milestones, each sitting out a 600-second deadline, and the suite hit its four-hour timeout. Three code paths — the one that deletes, the one that verifies, the one that catches what the others miss — were blind to the same object because a label had been stamped with an empty value. The component that only <em>observes</em> had been right all along. <strong>93 of 93, nothing failed and nothing skipped</strong>, in 2h48m, on the second attempt',
    links: [['Crucible 0.6.5', 'crucible-m6-5.html']],
  },
];

const gates = [
  {
    icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 7.5 9 4l5 3.5 5-3.5v9L14 20l-5-3.5L4 20Z"/><path d="M9 4v12.5M14 7.5V20"/></svg>',
    title: 'Reversibility is a CI gate (R8)',
    body: 'Every backup produced in CI is read back with a <strong>pinned upstream <code>restic</code></strong> binary — never our own tooling alone: <code>restic&nbsp;snapshots</code>, <code>restic&nbsp;check</code>, and a <code>restic&nbsp;dump</code> byte-comparison against the source. If our on-disk format ever drifts, the build breaks. Reversibility is a fact, not a slogan.',
  },
  {
    icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3v18M4 8l8-5 8 5-8 5-8-5Z"/><path d="m4 8 8 5 8-5"/></svg>',
    title: 'Metadata fidelity, four directions (R10)',
    body: 'A deterministic corpus — xattrs, POSIX ACLs, hardlinks, sparse files, setuid bits, unicode in <strong>NFC and NFD</strong>, paths past 255&nbsp;bytes — is backed up and restored, then diffed on SHA-256 content, allocated blocks, mode, xattrs, ACLs and hardlink classes, in <strong>all four</strong> backup/restore directions. This gates any change to the mover engine.',
  },
];

const guards = [
  ['Coverage gates', 'At least 80% on the controllers and 100% on the sanitization rules — an unreached rule fails CI, so no rule can rot untested.'],
  ['No flaky retries', 'No automatic retries — they hide the very races we cannot afford. Tests must pass under <code>-count=2</code>; two flakes within 7 days quarantines a test with a tracked fix SLA.'],
  ['Hardened supply chain', 'Images built with <strong>apko</strong> on <strong>Wolfi</strong> for a near-zero CVE surface, a 0-known-CVE scan gate, cosign signatures, an SBOM, and verified <strong>SLSA&nbsp;Build&nbsp;Level&nbsp;3</strong> provenance — plus an <strong>OpenVEX</strong> document re-generated and re-attested daily for the three most recent releases.'],
  ['Two-person review', 'CODEOWNERS forces two approvals on anything that touches encryption keys, the server-side tenancy derivation, or admission.'],
];

const title = 'Quality & testing — Crystal Backup';
const description =
  'How Crystal Backup guarantees the one thing that matters in a backup tool: a restore that works. A restore-centric test pyramid, upstream-restic reversibility gates, metadata fidelity, and a real-conditions crucible — all in the open.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${base}/favicon.svg`} />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#060912" />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://crystalbackup.github.io/CrystalBackup/quality/" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="orbs" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <!-- ───────────────────────── nav ───────────────────────── -->
    <header class="nav">
      <a class="brand" href={links.home} aria-label="Crystal Backup — home">
        <Crystal class="brand-mark" uid="nav" />
        <span class="brand-name">Crystal<span>Backup</span></span>
      </a>
      <nav class="nav-links">
        <a href={links.why}>Why</a>
        <a href={links.how}>How it works</a>
        <a href={links.compare}>Compare</a>
        <a href={links.roadmap}>Roadmap</a>
        <a href={links.docs}>Docs</a>
        <a class="current" href="#top" aria-current="page">Quality</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm" href={links.gh} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <span class="star">★</span> Star on GitHub
        </a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main id="top">
      <!-- ───────────────────────── intro ───────────────────────── -->
      <section class="section intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">Quality &amp; testing</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">
          A backup tool has one unforgivable failure: <span class="grad">a restore that doesn’t work.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="section-lead">
          So the whole test strategy is <strong>restore-centric</strong>: every backup produced in CI
          is restored and verified, and the reversibility promise is checked with the <strong>upstream
          <code>restic</code></strong> binary — never our own tooling alone. Here is exactly what stands
          between a change and your data, all of it public.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={links.testing} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Read the full test strategy →
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={links.crucible} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            The crucible harness
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── test pyramid ──────────────────── -->
      <section class="section">
        <p class="eyebrow">The pyramid</p>
        <h2>Five layers, each with a job</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">
          Fast checks run on every push; expensive, real-world checks gate releases and milestones.
          A change earns its way up.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="layers">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Harness</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Gate</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                layers.map((l) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="lname">{l.layer}</td>
                    <td set:html={l.harness} />
                    <td class="nowrap">{l.runs}</td>
                    <td><span class="gate">{l.gate}</span></td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── the hard gates ────────────────── -->
      <section class="section">
        <p class="eyebrow">The load-bearing gates</p>
        <h2>Two promises we refuse to fake</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          {
            gates.map((g) => (
              <article class="card gate-card">
                <span class="ico" set:html={g.icon} />
                <h3>{g.title}</h3>
                <p set:html={g.body} />
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── the crucible ──────────────────── -->
      <section class="section">
        <p class="eyebrow">Real conditions</p>
        <h2>The crucible</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">
          Kind and hostpath can’t vouch for Ceph’s xattr and ACL behaviour, or for a real object store.
          So the crucible provisions a <strong>disposable, real platform</strong> on Hetzner Cloud — RKE2
          HA, Rook-Ceph, Longhorn, an Object Storage bucket — and seeds tenant namespaces spanning the
          whole storage matrix: RWO and RWX, unmounted and detached volumes, non-snapshottable classes,
          and files with exotic metadata whose checksums are recorded in a <code>MANIFEST.sha256</code>.
        </p>
        <p class="flow-note">
          Its scenarios are <strong>labelled per milestone and only ever grow</strong>: the field
          acceptance gate at each delivery, the non-regression harness for everything already shipped,
          and a reproducible arena for issue reports. It runs on demand — it costs real money — never in
          the PR pipeline. Each milestone’s acceptance suite is written <strong>before</strong> its
          controllers, so a missing feature shows up as a failing real-world scenario, not a hopeful
          unit test.
        </p>
        <p class="section-foot">
          Included from M1: an <strong>off-cluster restore</strong> scenario that restores a backup
          straight from the bucket with nothing but the stock <code>restic</code> CLI and verifies it
          byte-for-byte against the seed — no operator, no custom resources, no surviving cluster
          needed. See <a href={links.crucible} target="_blank" rel="noopener">test/crucible</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ─────────────────── the published reports ────────────── -->
      <section class="section">
        <p class="eyebrow">The evidence</p>
        <h2>Every acceptance run, published in full</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">
          No milestone shipped without passing its crucible run on real infrastructure. The
          reports below are the raw output of those runs — check by check, including the
          <strong>skips</strong> and the <strong>defects each round found</strong>. Writing the M5
          suite alone surfaced three advertised features that were completely inert on real
          storage. That is the argument for reading them rather than a summary.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="layers reports">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Milestone</th><th>What the run covered</th><th>Report</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                reports.map((r) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="lname">{r.milestone}</td>
                    <td set:html={r.covered} />
                    <td class="rlinks">
                      {r.links.map(([label, file]) => (
                        <a href={`${base}/reports/${file}`}>{label}</a>
                      ))}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="section-foot">
          The oracle in those runs is <strong>independent</strong>: every claim about a repository
          is re-checked by a throwaway Job running the plain upstream <code>restic</code> CLI
          against the same repository, credentials and key the operator used. A controller that
          both writes and reports the same wrong thing cannot make a check pass.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────── the guardrails ────────────────── -->
      <section class="section">
        <p class="eyebrow">The guardrails</p>
        <h2>What keeps it honest between the gates</h2>
        <div class="guards">
          {
            guards.map(([h, b]) => (
              <div class="guard">
                <h3>{h}</h3>
                <p set:html={b} />
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ───────────────────────── cta ───────────────────────── -->
      <section class="cta">
        <h2>Every check is in the open</h2>
        <p>The test strategy, the coverage gates and the crucible are all public — read them, run them, poke holes in them.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={links.testing} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Testing &amp; Definition of Done →
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={links.gh} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <span class="star">★</span> Star / watch on GitHub
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <!-- ───────────────────────── footer ────────────────────── -->
    <footer class="footer">
      <div class="foot-brand">
        <Crystal class="brand-mark" uid="foot" />
        <span class="brand-name">Crystal<span>Backup</span></span>
      </div>
      <nav class="foot-links">
        <a href={links.home}>Home</a>
        <a href={links.docs}>Documentation</a>
        <a href={links.testing} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Testing &amp; DoD</a>
        <a href={links.security} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Security &amp; tenancy</a>
        <a href={links.crucible} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Crucible</a>
        <a href={links.adr} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Decision records</a>
        <a href={links.license} target="_blank" rel="noopener">License</a>
        <a href={links.gh} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
      </nav>
      <p class="foot-fine">
        Apache-2.0 · built in the open with AI assistance · provided as-is, without warranty — use
        it at your own risk.
      </p>
    </footer>

    <style>
      /* Page-specific styling; the shared design system lives in src/styles/site.css. */
      .nav-links a.current { color: var(--text); font-weight: 650; }
      .intro { padding-top: clamp(40px, 8vw, 84px); }
      .page-title {
        font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem);
        font-weight: 850;
        max-width: 20ch;
        margin: 14px 0 0;
      }

      table.layers { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; min-width: 640px; }
      .layers th, .layers td { padding: 14px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--brd); vertical-align: top; }
      .layers thead th { font-size: 0.9rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--muted); background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02); }
      .layers tbody td { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.96rem; }
      .layers td.lname { color: var(--text); font-weight: 650; white-space: nowrap; }
      .layers td.nowrap { white-space: nowrap; }
      .layers tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
      .layers tbody tr:last-child { background: linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(63, 200, 245, 0.08), transparent); }
      .gate {
        display: inline-block;
        font-size: 0.78rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        color: #b9e8fb;
        background: rgba(63, 200, 245, 0.1);
        border: 1px solid rgba(63, 200, 245, 0.22);
        padding: 3px 10px;
        border-radius: 999px;
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      /* The reports table reuses .layers; only the link cell needs its own rules. */
      .reports tbody tr:last-child { background: none; }
      .reports td.rlinks { white-space: nowrap; }
      .reports td.rlinks a {
        display: block;
        color: var(--cyan);
        font-weight: 650;
        font-size: 0.92rem;
        text-decoration: none;
      }
      .reports td.rlinks a + a { margin-top: 6px; }
      .reports td.rlinks a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

      .gate-card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
      .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }

      .guards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 18px; margin-top: 34px; }
      .guard {
        border: 1px solid var(--brd);
        background: var(--panel);
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        padding: 22px 24px;
        border-left: 3px solid var(--cyan);
      }
      .guard h3 { font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 8px; }
      .guard p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }
      .guard strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 650; }

      @media (max-width: 900px) {
        .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
        .guards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
